title: metarecord-list panel
tags: Panel Metarecord
kind: reference
aliases: metarecord-list columns metarecords quick-filter query-editor
summary: The metarecords a query selects, as a table or a grid: a finder and a query in two AND-ed languages above, chosen columns, a cursor that is the workspace's selection, and bulk edits.

Open it with `panel:open here metarecord-list`
(<<key "panel:open here metarecord-list" "t l">>). It lists the metarecords of the
workspace's repository that match its query — all of them while the query is empty —
and runs that query as soon as it is shown, without waiting for an `Enter`.

!! Three search fields

From top to bottom. What runs is the conjunction of the three: the finder ''and'' the
simplified query ''and'' the normal DSL — an empty one adds nothing, and all three
empty list everything.

* The ''finder'' (<<key "metarecord-list:focus finder" "/">>) narrows the list as you type, half a second after the last key. Each word must appear, in order, in one of the fields it searches ([[Ordered substring matching]]): `scien fic` finds `science-fiction`, `conf gui` a path `…/config/…/gui/…`. Those fields are named at the right of the box — `mfr_path`, `label` and `name` unless configured otherwise.
* The ''simplified query'' (<<key "metarecord-list:focus simplified" "p e">>) takes the [[Simplified query language]]. `Enter` or ''Apply'' runs it. The line under it shows what it expands to in the normal DSL, as you type it: read-only, but it can be selected and copied — into the normal DSL, say, to edit it by hand. The arrow at the end of the simplified field (`▾` / `▸`, or `metarecord-list:toggle expansion`) hides or shows that line, and the choice is kept with the workspace's layout.
* The ''normal DSL'' takes the query language itself ([[Writing queries]]) and is AND-ed with the simplified query: `(<expansion>) AND (<normal DSL>)` is what is sent to the daemon. So a filter is added in either language without giving up what the other holds — a folder listed by `metarecord-list:folder` is narrowed with a tag typed in the simplified field ([[Why the two query fields are AND-ed]]).

`Enter` in any of them applies and leaves the field, so that the panel's keys work
again; <<key "metarecord-list:apply finder stay" Shift+Enter>> applies and stays. An
error is shown under the field it comes from — the simplified query's (its
expansion, or the parse of that expansion) under the simplified query, the normal
DSL's under the normal DSL. The finder and the simplified query each
keep a history, recorded when you apply and not at each debounced run
([[Input history]]).

|!Key |!Does |
|<<key "metarecord-list:focus finder" "/">>, <<key "metarecord-list:focus simplified" "p e">>, <<key "metarecord-list:focus normal" "p q">> |edit the finder, the simplified query, the normal DSL, keeping what it holds |
|<<key "metarecord-list:focus finder clear" "Shift+f">>, <<key "metarecord-list:focus simplified clear" "p Shift+e">>, <<key "metarecord-list:focus normal clear" "p Shift+q">> |empty it first |
|<<key "metarecord-list:clear-all" "p u">> |empty the three and list everything |

Going to one field, or clearing it, leaves the other as it is: both are always part
of the query.

A query written by a command arrives in the normal DSL, so that it can be edited or
combined: `metarecord-list:folder` (<<key "metarecord-list:folder" "g f">>, the
selection's folder — [[Cross-panel selection]]), `path-ref:list-refs`
([[path-ref panel]]), and `metarecord-list:query`, which takes `duplicates` (the files
byte-identical to the selection, `same(mfr_duplicate_group, <uuid>)` —
[[Duplicates]]) or `orphans` (`orphan = true`, what the last detection marked —
[[Finding and clearing orphans]]). Each takes a last argument: `replace`, the default
and what their keys do, empties the simplified query, so that the command's query is
the whole query; `and` keeps it, AND-ed with the command's query (`metarecord-list:folder
here and`: the folder, narrowed by the simplified query in place). Either way the
command's query replaces what the normal DSL held. `folder:open` always replaces.

!! Columns

The ''columns'' field below the queries chooses what the table shows; ''Apply'' or
`Enter` redraws it, without asking the daemon again for the rows. Columns are
separated by spaces or commas, and an empty field brings the configured ones back. A
column that does not parse is reported under the field and the previous columns stay.

* `name` — the field's values, every row of a field that has several, separated by commas.
* `name:aspect` — one aspect of a `tree_ref` value: `:path` (the path from the root), `:value` (the node's own name), `:parent` (the parent's uuid), `:raw` (parent and name, the default). The same names as in a query ([[Field aspects]]).
* `name>sub` — follows a `ref` to its target and shows the target's `sub` field: `tag>label`, or `tag>path:path` for an aspect of it. One `>` only. A target that no longer exists shows the uuid.
* `a | b` — the first of the two that has a value: `tag>label | tag>path:value`. Parentheses may group it.
* `&uuid`, `&version` — the metarecord's own uuid and [[version|Metarecord version]], which are not fields.

Clicking a header sorts by it — ascending, then descending, then not at all. The
daemon sorts the field's stored values, so a `tag>label` column sorts by the `tag`
uuids; `&` columns do not sort ([[Pagination and sorting]]). Dragging a header's right
edge resizes the column.

''Order by'', beside the columns, orders the list by an `int` field instead: it numbers
the rows that have no position, sorts by it, and
<<key "metarecord-list:order move up" Alt+↑>> / <<key "metarecord-list:order move down" Alt+↓>>
move the row under the cursor by rewriting it ([[Ordering a list]]).

!! Rows

The list loads a page of 100 rows (`[page-size]`, [[GUI configuration]];
`metarecord-list:set page-size <n>` for this workspace) and the next one when the
scroll or the cursor reaches the end; `metarecord-list:page-next` asks for it. The
footer gives `loaded/total` — the total is asked from the daemon apart, after the
first page ([[Sorting and postings]]) — and how many metarecords are checked.

`metarecord-list:set mode grid` shows a thumbnail per metarecord instead of the table,
named by the path ([[Display modes]]);
<<key "metarecord-list:toggle mode" "v g">> switches between the two.

A row's colour says something about its file:

* ''purple'' — the file is gone: the metarecord is an orphan ([[Orphans in the GUI]]);
* ''amber'' — the file is not watched, so a change to it would not be recorded, or it sits on a volume that is not mounted ([[Checking whether a path is watched]], [[Unmounted volumes in the GUI]]).

The reason is in the tooltip — also on a watched row, which is not coloured. A
metarecord without `mfr_path` is not a file and is never marked; neither is one whose
answer has not arrived, since unknown must not read as unwatched.

When the list cannot be read — the daemon down, a query refused — the reason takes
the place of the rows and of the footer: a failed read never looks like an empty
result. The list re-reads itself when something in the GUI changes metarecords, and
re-reads the paths of its rows whenever the daemon reports a change in the repository.

!! The cursor and the selection

* <<key "cursor:next self" "↓">> / <<key "cursor:prev self" "↑">>, <<key "cursor:first self" Home>> / <<key "cursor:last self" End>> (the last row ''loaded'') — move the cursor. The row under it is the workspace's selection, which the other panels follow; the arrows work from the finder too.
* <<key "panel:open other viewer" "Enter or →">>, or the `↗` button at the start of a row or under a card ([[Opening a row]]) — show it in the [[viewer panel]] of the other slot; the button is greyed on a metarecord without a file, and the key refuses it. <<key "panel:open other metarecord-detail" Shift+Enter>>, or the `ⓘ` button — in the [[metarecord-detail panel]]. After `b` (<<key "panel:open new viewer" "n t f">>) in a new workspace instead, after `i` (<<key "panel:open here viewer" "t f">>) in place of the list ([[open targets|Panel slots and layout]]).
* <<key "metarecord-list:select toggle" Space>> — check the row; `metarecord-list:select all` checks every loaded row, <<key "metarecord-list:select none" "Ctrl+Shift+a">> unchecks everything ([[Cross-panel selection]]).
* <<key "view:refresh self" "Ctrl+r">> — run the query again; the cursor stays on its metarecord.
* <<key "clipboard:copy self" Ctrl+c>>, <<key "clipboard:cut self" Ctrl+x>> — put the files of the checked metarecords, or the file of the row under the cursor, in the file clipboard, to paste in the [[file-manager panel]] ([[Global GUI variables]]).
* <<key "clipboard:paste self" Ctrl+v>> — paste the file clipboard inside the folder under the cursor, or beside its file ([[clipboard:paste]]).
* <<key "item:delete" Delete>>, `file:trash`, <<key "metarecord:delete" Shift+Delete>> — remove the selected metarecord, its file, or the metarecord alone ([[Sending files to the trash]]).

A right click acts on the row clicked: the metarecord's actions, ''Add to the
selection'', and the file's actions when it has one ([[Context menus]]).

!! Editing

The `m` keys edit the fields of the selected metarecord, and of the checked ones or
of every match, from the command input; ''Edit / delete…'' in the footer
(`metarecord-list:open-bulk-edit`) opens a form doing the same with the mouse. Both
are described in [[Editing metarecords in the GUI]].

!! As a value picker

Opened to pick a metarecord ([[Value picker]]), the panel puts the focus in the
finder, and a row's right-click menu starts with ''Pick this metarecord''.

!! Its variables

The panel keeps its state in [[workspace variables|Workspace variables]], which a
script can read and set.

|!Variable |!Holds |
|`metarecord-list:finder` |the finder's text |
|`metarecord-list:finder-fields` |the fields the finder searches, as `field:path` or `field:value`; a bare `field` is searched as a path when it is a `tree_ref` |
|`metarecord-list:query` |the simplified query |
|`metarecord-list:normal-query` |the normal DSL field |
|`metarecord-list:show-expansion` |`false` when the expansion line is hidden |
|`metarecord-list:columns`, `:column-widths` |the columns, as a list, and their widths |
|`metarecord-list:page-size` |the page size, when changed |
|`metarecord-list:order-field` |the `int` field the list is ordered by, or `null` ([[Ordering a list]]) |
|`metarecord-list:mode` |`table` or `grid` ([[Display modes]]) |
|`metarecord-list:effective-query` |what was last run, finder included, as [[Query IR]] — what `metarecord:bulk` acts on |
|`metarecord-list:effective-query-text` |the same as DSL text (empty for everything), which [[mf gui query]] prints |
|`metarecord-list:sort` |the order it was run in, as the query body's `sort` (empty for none) — what a [[Slideshow]] of `query` plays it in |
|`metarecord-list:base-query-text` |the query without the finder — the simplified query's expansion AND the normal DSL — as DSL text |

The two query variables (`:query`, `:normal-query`) are the list's ''state'', not a record of it: written by another
panel, a command or a script, they are put in the zones and run — at once when the
list is on screen, when it next is otherwise. A burst of writes runs once, on the final
state; a write of what the list already shows, or of what it wrote itself, runs
nothing, and a query being typed but not yet applied is left alone. Nor does a read of
the variables that overlaps one of the list's own writes: begun before it, it holds the
query the list ''had'' — following it put the previous query back and ran it — so it
is dropped, and the variables re-read once the writes are done (as in the
[[file-manager panel]]). A copy of the
workspace's variables therefore shows the same list ([[Cross-panel selection]]).

`metarecord-list:insert <zone> <text>` writes into one of the four fields (`finder`,
`simplified`, `normal`, `columns`) at the caret, and `metarecord-list:apply <zone>`
runs it; each takes a final `stay` to leave the focus where it is. A command of yours
that writes a query and runs it is those two in a row ([[User commands]]).
